## Blocking counterexample at the subject commit

The witness ledger assigns false responsibility across independent closure items.
`tools/claims.py:1863-1864` collapses revalidations to transition IDs, discarding the
duty each revalidation closes. At lines 1874-1875 it combines that transition-wide
set with an acceptance's item-specific challenge and calls the acceptance refuted.

A transition X closes duty A as item 0 and duty B as item 1. Codex accepts item 1;
the owner challenges item 1. No revalidation of B exists. The ledger initially
reports no refuted acceptance, consistent with shipped W3's rule that a challenge
alone is insufficient. Add a transition revalidating A only, referencing X. The
ledger now reports codex's acceptance of B as refuted: "the accepted closure was
challenged, then revalidated". Only A was revalidated. No record or acceptance
of B changed. The fixture model reports no problems.

This fails D's requirement for responsibility for acceptances later refuted: the
projection attributes a refutation to the wrong closure. It is reproducible at the
exact named subject, independent of the later removal of the implementation.

The reproducer uses #170's signed ephemeral fixture records. No counter replay is
stubbed or needed: it exercises the obligation challenge/revalidation branch.
Its --control option changes only the loaded ledger function in memory to retain
(transition, duty) pairs and match the accepted item's duty. That control passes;
no repository implementation was patched.

